title: How it works
tags: Contents

A DecentraWiki wiki goes round a loop with three steps.

!!! 1. Read

A published wiki is a [[TiddlyWiki|https://tiddlywiki.com/]] page on [[IPFS|https://ipfs.tech/]]. It opens at an address like `ipns://k51…`, which shows the wiki's newest version, or at the IPFS address of one version, `ipfs://bafy…`, or at a name such as `decentrawiki.eth` that points to it. It reads like any wiki: search, links, tags, a story of open pages.

!!! 2. Edit

The pen on the page, and the pencil on every tiddler, open the DecentraWiki editor at `editor.decentrawiki.eth` with a copy of the wiki. The copy lives in your browser and is kept as you type. You can leave and come back to it; the editor holds every wiki you have edited.

!!! 3. Publish

The editor's ''Publish'' button builds the wiki as a static page, computes its IPFS address (its CID), pins it with a service you chose, and points the wiki's address at it. The new version opens at its own address and at the wiki's address, with the pen on it, and the loop starts again.

!!! What changes, and what does not

Publishing never overwrites anything: every version has its own IPFS address, made from its content, so that address always shows exactly the version it was made from. What moves is the wiki's address, an IPNS name: whoever holds its key points it at a newer version. An ENS name can point at the wiki's address once and then follow every version. See [[A wiki's address]] and [[Maintain a wiki]].